WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.624
A practical private dashboard can run on a local server, workstation, or small cluster,

00:00:05.624 --> 00:00:07.992
depending on scale and availability needs.

00:00:07.992 --> 00:00:12.333
Collectors or agents receive events from cameras, endpoints, network tools, sensors,

00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:13.122
and applications.

00:00:13.122 --> 00:00:17.068
APIs support controlled integration when direct collection is not appropriate.

00:00:17.068 --> 00:00:21.803
A message queue can buffer events and separate producers from downstream processing.

00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:26.539
This helps absorb bursts and lets services recover without immediately losing data.

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:29.696
A database or event store retains normalized records,

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:34.431
while larger artifacts such as video clips may use separate storage paths

00:00:34.431 --> 00:00:36.798
with tighter access and retention controls.

00:00:36.798 --> 00:00:40.744
Event normalization maps different source formats into a consistent structure.

00:00:40.744 --> 00:00:45.480
Common fields may include event time, source, category, severity, confidence, asset, location,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.058
status, and evidence link.

00:00:47.058 --> 00:00:51.793
Preserve the original record or a reference to it so normalization does

00:00:51.793 --> 00:00:54.161
not erase details needed for investigation.

00:00:54.161 --> 00:00:59.686
The dashboard frontend should query controlled services rather than connect directly to every source.

00:00:59.686 --> 00:01:04.421
This reduces credential sprawl and creates one place to enforce authorization, pagination,

00:01:04.421 --> 00:01:05.999
filtering, and audit logging.

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:10.735
Service accounts and API keys should have narrow privileges and protected storage.

00:01:10.735 --> 00:01:15.470
Design the flow from collection through review: source, collector, queue, normalizer, store,

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.443
dashboard, notification, and analyst action.

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:21.784
At each step, identify what happens during delay, duplication, malformed input,

00:01:21.784 --> 00:01:24.151
storage failure, or loss of connectivity.

00:01:24.151 --> 00:01:26.913
Event identifiers and timestamps help prevent confusion.

00:01:26.913 --> 00:01:30.860
Privacy-aware architecture keeps sensitive content close to its legitimate purpose.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:35.200
Store clips separately from ordinary alert metadata, limit thumbnails where possible,

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.146
minimize personal data, encrypt appropriate paths, and avoid public exposure.

00:01:39.146 --> 00:01:45.460
Architecture should make safe handling the default rather than relying on every user to remember policy.
